# Architecture

Inspect the existing implementation before introducing new architecture. Prefer extending existing modules over creating parallel systems. Prefer small, traceable changes over broad rewrites. Do not refactor unrelated code while implementing a feature unless explicitly requested.

## Module boundaries

```
apps/web                  React manager UI — presentation only
packages/math-engine      Pure deterministic formulas — no I/O
packages/plan-domain      Plan lifecycle, fingerprints, adoption
netlify/functions         Server-side integration boundary
```

- `packages/math-engine` is pure. It MUST NOT import React, Netlify, a database client, storage, or network code. Nothing may import from `apps/web` into a package.
- Math-engine computes. Plan-domain assembles and verifies. UI renders. Never move lifecycle logic into math-engine.
- Plan-domain may depend on `@wings/math-engine` and `PlanStore`. It MUST NOT depend on React, Netlify, or any concrete database client.
- Screens edit draft parts. They NEVER build a `PlanVersionRecord`, input fingerprint, or calculation snapshot — `builder.ts` in `@wings/plan-domain` is the only producer. A screen that builds its own record or hash silently overwrites inputs owned by other screens.

## Plan lifecycle

An ADOPTED plan version is immutable. Enforced in two places — both must stay:

1. Reject an incoming draft that targets an adopted plan.
2. Reject a write whose ID matches an already-adopted stored record.

The second check exists because the first alone allows a caller to reuse an adopted ID and overwrite it — the fresh draft does not *target* an adopted plan, so check 1 passes. Do not remove check 2 as redundant.

A reforecast is a new child plan version with a new version ID and `parentPlanVersionId`. It never mutates its parent.

`hash.ts` fingerprints must stay stable across object-key ordering. Any canonicalization change invalidates stored fingerprints — treat as breaking.

Adoption verifies four things. Removing any one is a correctness regression:

1. Current canonical input fingerprint matches the saved draft fingerprint.
2. It matches the fingerprint on every material calculation snapshot.
3. It matches `expectedInputHash` — the fingerprint of the plan the manager was reviewing when they clicked Adopt. Checks 1 and 2 verify data consistency; only this one verifies the manager saw the version being adopted.
4. Every material feasibility gap carries a current, non-stale acknowledgment.

Plus: every section marked `requiredForAdoption` is complete. Section requirements are evaluated against the enabled program objective set.

Adoption never silently recomputes. It refuses and reports.

Feasibility acknowledgments store a snapshot plus `gapFingerprint`. If the gap changes, the acknowledgment is stale and adoption fails with `FEASIBILITY_ACK_STALE`. Constraint types: `CAPACITY`, `COST`, `REACHABILITY`, `ALLOCATION`, `OTHER`.

Route adoption errors using structured context on `PlanAdoptionError`. Do not parse error strings in the UI.

## UI architecture

Six primary screens: Campaign Setup, Path to Victory, Program & Budget, Adopt Plan, Command Center, Reforecast.

Progressive disclosure — three levels:

- **Level 1** — the answer (status, goal, gap). Nothing a manager would not act on today.
- **Level 2** — cause and response in plain English sentences, not formulas.
- **Level 3** — audit (formulas, metric keys, assumption keys, versions).

390px is the primary layout. Full-width buttons, 44px touch targets, single-column cards, no horizontal overflow.

Status: ON TRACK (green), WATCH (amber), AT RISK (red), NO DATA (hollow gray ring — never performance-positive). Engine `UNAVAILABLE` displays as NO DATA with a hollow gray ring — never a filled dot, never green.

Every status is tappable and names its triggering rule.

No localStorage outside `src/storage/`. No health scores, gauges, or composite status.

Wait for confirmation before changing adoption gates, immutability checks, canonical fingerprints, or the math-engine purity boundary.
